4 has 2 points, column 5 has 1 point. That's total 10 points. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so with 4 sunny lines we can cover at most 4 points in column 2, which is enough; at most 4 points in column 3, but we only need 3; column 4 at most 4 points, need 2; column 5 at most 4 points, need 1. So coverage is possible in principle. However, we need to assign points to lines such that each line's points across columns are collinear and sunny.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each must pass through a distinct point in column 2 (since column 2 has 4 points). So each line will be determined by its point in column 2 and also must pass through a point in column 1? Actually we have a vertical line covering column 1, so we don't need to assign column 1 points to sunny lines. So each sunny line can be assigned to a point in column 2, and then we need to assign points in columns 3,4,5 to each line such that they are collinear.\n\nThus we need to find 4 sunny lines that each pass through a distinct point in column 2 (i.e., (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4)), and also collectively cover all points in columns 3,4,5. Let's try to see if possible.\n\nWe need to cover points in column 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). These must be assigned to some of the 4 lines. Since each line can cover at most one point in column 3, we need at least 3 lines to cover column 3. That's okay, we have 4 lines.\n\nSimilarly column 4: (4,1),(4,2) need at most 2 lines.\n\nColumn 5: (5,1) need at most 1 line.\n\nThus we need to assign each point in columns 3-5 to some line, ensuring each line's assigned points across columns are collinear.\n\nWe can try to construct such lines.\n\nLet’s denote lines L_i for i=1..4, each passing through (2,i) (i=1..4). We'll try to assign points:\n\n- L1 passes through (2,1). It could also pass through (3,2) (slope (2-1)/(3-2)=1) => line y = x - 1? Actually line through (2,1) and (3,2) has slope 1, equation y = x - 1. At x=4, y=3 (point (4,3) not in set). At x=5, y=4 (not in set). 