ugh (2,4) slope (4-2)/(2-1) = 2, allowed: line y = 2x? Actually passes through (1,2): y - 2 = 2 (x - 1) => y = 2x. At x=3, y = 6 (not in set). Through (3,1) slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, allowed: line y = -0.5 x + 2.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 2.5 = 1.5 (not integer). Through (3,3) slope (3-2)/(3-1) = 1/2 = 0.5, allowed: line y = 0.5 x + 1.5. At x=2, y = 1 + 1.5 = 2.5 (not integer). Through (4,2) slope (2-2)/(4-1) = 0, not allowed. Through (5,1) slope (1-2)/(5-1) = -1/4 = -0.25, allowed: line y = -0.25 x + 2.25. At x=2, y = -0.5 + 2.25 = 1.75 (not integer). So anchor (1,2) seems not able to cover integer points in other columns except maybe (2,4) which is okay.\n\n- Anchor (1,3): lines through (2,1) slope (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed: line y = -2x + 5. At x=3, y = -6 + 5 = -1 (no). Through (2,2) slope (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. Through (2,4) slope (4-3)/(2-1) = 1, allowed: line y = x + 2. At x=3, y = 5 (no). Through (3,1) slope (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. Through (3,3) slope (3-3)/(3-1) = 0, not allowed. Through (4,2) slope (2-3)/(4-1) = -1/3 ≈ -0.333, allowed: line y = -1/3 x + 10/3? Actually passes through (1,3): y - 3 = -1/3 (x - 1) => y = -1/3 x + 10/3. At x=2, y = -2/3 + 10/3 = 8/3 ≈ 2.667 (not integer). Through (5,1) slope (1-3)/(5-1) = -2/4 = -0.5, allowed: line y = -0.5 x + 3.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5 (not integer). So anchor (1,3) can cover (2,4) maybe.\n\n- Anchor (1,5): lines through (2,1) slope (1-5)/(2-1) = -4, allowed: line y = -4x + 9. At x=3, y = -12 + 9 = -3 (no). Through (2,2) slope (2-5)/(2-1) = -3, allowed: line y = -3x + 8. At x=3, y = -9 + 8 = -1 (no). Through (2,4) slope (4-5)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. Through (3,1) slope (1-5)/(3-1) = -4/2 = -2, allowed: line y = -2x + 7. At x=2, y = -4 + 7 = 3 (point (2,3) not needed? Actually (2,3) is covered by anti-diagonal line x+y=5, so we don't need to cover it again). At x=4, y = -8 + 7 = -1 (no). Through (3,3) slope (3-5)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. Through (4,2) slope (2-5)/(4-1) = -3/3