(3,2) and (5,2) not allowed.\n\n- L1: through (3,2) and (5,2) not allowed.\n\nThus L1 must be (3,2)-(4,3) or (3,2)-(5,? maybe (5,?)). Let's try L1: (3,2) and (5,? ) maybe (5,? ) not in set. (5,2) is horizontal, not allowed. So L1 must be (3,2)-(4,3). That covers those two.\n\nNow remaining points: (3,3),(4,2),(5,2). We need to cover them with one sunny line (since we have only 2 sunny lines total). That's impossible because a line can cover at most 2 points (if they are collinear). Let's see if any line passes through all three: (3,3),(4,2),(5,2). Check if (3,3),(4,2) slope = -1, diagonal, not allowed. So cannot. So fails.\n\nThus this arrangement fails.\n\nTry using 2 vertical lines and 1 diagonal line as non-sunny. For example, vertical x=1, x=2, diagonal x+y=6 (since n+1=6). Diagonal x+y=6 passes through (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). Let's see coverage:\n\n- x=1 covers (1,1)-(1,5).\n- x=2 covers (2,1)-(2,4).\n- diagonal x+y=6 covers (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). Some overlap.\n\nNow covered points: (1,1)-(1,5), (2,1)-(2,4), (3,3),(4,2),(5,1). Uncovered points: (3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,3),(5,2). That's 5 points left, 2 sunny lines. Points: (3,1),(3,2) column 3; (4,1),(4,3) column 4; (5,2) column 5. We need to cover them with 2 sunny lines. Let's try to pair:\n\n- L1: through (3,1) and (4,3): slope = (3-1)/(4-3) = 2, allowed. L1 covers (3,1),(4,3). Also passes through (5,5) not needed.\n\n- L2: we need to cover (3,2),(4,1),(5,2). Let's see if we can find a line covering (3,2) and (5,2): horizontal y=2, not allowed. (3,2) and (4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-3) = -1, diagonal, not allowed. (3,2) and (5,2) horizontal, not allowed. So we cannot cover (3,2) with any other point using a sunny line. So we need a line for (3,2) alone, leaving (4,1) and (5,2) uncovered. So fails.\n\nTry different L1: (3,2) and (4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-3) = -1, diagonal, not allowed. (3,2) and (5,2) horizontal, not allowed. So (3,2) must be alone.\n\nThus k=2 fails for n=5.\n\nThus k=2 seems impossible