ints: x=1 covers 5 points, x=2 covers 4 points, x+y=5 covers 4 points (some overlap). Let's count distinct points covered: x=1: (1,1)-(1,5). x=2: (2,1)-(2,4). x+y=5: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). Union: (1,1)-(1,5) all covered; (2,1)-(2,4) all covered; (3,2) covered; (4,1) covered. So covered points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(3,2),(4,1). That's 11 points. Uncovered: (2,5?) not in set; (3,1) sum=4; (3,3) sum=6; (4,2) sum=6; (5,1) sum=6; (3,1) sum=4; (2,? ) maybe (3,1) not covered; (3,3) not covered; (4,2) not covered; (5,1) not covered; (2,? ) maybe (3,1) sum=4; (2,? ) (2,? ) all covered; (1,? ) all covered. So uncovered points: (3,1),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). That's 4 points. We have 2 sunny lines left. As before, we can cover (4,2) and (3,1) with a sunny line slope 1. Then left (3,3) and (5,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line. So fails.\n\nTry vertical lines x=1, x=3, and slope -1 line x+y=5. x=1 covers (1,1)-(1,5). x=3 covers (3,1)-(3,3). x+y=5 covers (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). Union: (1,1)-(1,5); (3,1)-(3,3); (2,3); (4,1). Also (2,1),(2,2),(2,4) not covered; (3,2) covered; (3,3) covered; (4,2) not covered; (5,1) not covered; (2,3) covered; (3,1) covered; (2,2) not covered; (2,1) not covered; (2,4) not covered; (4,1) covered. So uncovered: (2,1),(2,2),(2,4),(4,2),(5,1). That's 5 points. We have 2 sunny lines left, cannot cover 5 points.\n\nTry using horizontal lines y=1, y=2, and slope -1 line x+y=5. y=1 covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1). y=2 covers (1,2),(2,2),(3,2),(4,2). x+y=5 covers (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). Union: (1,1)-(1,5?) Actually (1,5) not covered; (1,1),(1,2),(1,3?) not covered; (1,4) covered; (1,5) not covered; (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) covered; (2,4) not covered; (3,1),(3,2) covered; (3,3) not covered; (4,1),(4,2) covered; (5,1) covered; (5,? ) maybe (5,? ) only (5,1) covered. So uncovered: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,3?) not in set; (2,? ) maybe (2,? ) (2,5) not in set; (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) Actually (3,3) su